Three things, in descending order of how much they should move the scope call.

## 1. `AD5` is CONFIRMED by execution. The magnitude is "all but one, from any height."

Review's §5 named the fixture and said *"both should be red today if this reading is right."* I built it as a
scratch diagnostic in the pipeline package, ran it, and deleted it. Composition exactly as specified: P/D
model, `[sat,TA]`, saturation `Enabled: true, Live: false` carrying topology (`AcceleratorName`, `Cost`,
`Role`, `ReplicaCount`), TA live and binding, prefill `TotalDemand: 0` with supply untouched, decode's demand
set to exactly cover its own replicas as a control, no `MinReplicas`.

| start (each role) | cost-aware | greedy | decode (control) |
|---|---|---|---|
| 2 | pf = **1** | pf = **1** | dc = 2 ✓ |
| 4 | pf = **1** | pf = **1** | dc = 4 ✓ |
| 8 | pf = **1** | pf = **1** | dc = 8 ✓ |

Decode holds at its starting count at every height, in both optimizers — so this is not a global scale-down,
it is role-selective, which is the signature §2 predicted. Prefill goes to **1 regardless of where it
started**, in a single pass: an 8-replica prefill tier sheds 7 replicas in one reconcile.

That is worse than *"sheds prefill to ~1 replica"* reads. "~1" invites "loses a replica or two"; the measured
behaviour is that the starting size does not matter at all. For the scope call: the cost of leaving this open
is not proportional to fleet size, it is the whole prefill tier minus one, every time the window opens.

## 2. Correction to my own handoff — review is right, and I was wrong

`plan__ta-anchor-ad5-cites-main-not-pr2-and-vgup-already-landed.md` §2 offered **two** live paths to zero
prefill GPUs, one being *"missing prefill key → empty ballot → `binder < 0`."* **That path does not fire, and
my saying so was wrong.** Verified myself rather than relayed:

- `aggregateRoleCapacities:960-967` keys the map by role and returns nil only for non-disaggregated models, so
  a P/D model **has** a prefill key.
- The `RolePrefill` guard at `analyzer.go:364` scopes only the decode ITL/OL accumulators; the
  `VariantCapacity` append at `:398-408` is outside it and unconditional, so a live prefill variant carries
  `PerReplicaCapacity > 0` and `prcForVariant` returns positive — no `prc <= 0` abstain.

So the ballot is a real vote honestly valued zero, `combineVotes(up=true)` returns exactly `(0, validIndex)`,
and **a hold predicate keyed on abstain would never fire** — review's §3 placement diagnosis is correct and my
suggested placement was unreachable. Please treat their §3 as authoritative over my §2 on this point.

**One refinement in the other direction, which strengthens rather than weakens the finding.** The zero is more
categorical than either of us wrote. `distributeDemandByRole:918-940` excludes prefill *by construction*, and
`:478` / `:483` are its **only two call sites** — the sole source of both demand maps — while
`aggregateRoleCapacities`'s own comment confirms it no longer sums per-variant demand. So TA reports prefill
`TotalDemand == 0` **for every P/D model, always** — not as a data-dependent edge case. The exposure is
unconditional whenever TA is the only live voter.

This also pins the unmasking mechanism exactly: pre-`VG-up`, a dead-but-`Enabled` saturation still voted, and
its *stale* prefill demand won the `up=true` max — wrong, but nonzero. `VG-up` removed the only nonzero prefill
voter, leaving TA's structural zero. Both behaviours are wrong; only the new one collapses.

Consequence worth stating plainly for the Type 1: because prefill demand has no denominator today (both terms
are decode-rate-denominated, per `distributeDemandByRole`'s own comment), **the fix cannot be a pipeline
patch.** Giving prefill a demand number is a modelling decision — how prefill demand is denominated — which is
the designer's and Dean's, not a coder's. Per plan §7.1 I am not deciding it.

## 3. What I did NOT confirm — please don't read the table as more than it is

- **Only the steady-state path is confirmed.** Review's §5 also asked to *"repeat with rescale enabled and the
  group contended for the second path."* I did not do that. My greedy run used unlimited constraints, so it
  was almost certainly not contended and very likely did not reach `reclaimRole`. **`reclaimRole`
  (`rescale.go:404-427`) remains PLAUSIBLE-by-reading, not confirmed** — including its "newly unmasked by
  `VG-up`" attribution, which is the half that matters for whether PR-2 authored any of this.
- **I did not instrument which internal path each optimizer took.** The table shows two *optimizers* reproducing
  it, not two *gates*.
- **The floor at 1 is unexplained.** §2's `reclaimRole` bullet predicts the *whole* allocation is reclaimed
  (0 when `minReplicas` is unset). I measured 1, never 0, at every height. Either a clamp intervened or the run
  took the `scaleDownRoleIterated` path. I did not chase it. It matters only for "degraded vs outage" wording —
  not for the scope call — so I stopped rather than widen unasked.
- I did not touch the second observability site (`cost_aware_optimizer.go:350-367`). Review's reading that a
  sizing-only fix leaves the operator series at 0 is consistent with what I saw, but I did not measure the gauge.

